From e0d4ed0f624ef9c650dfb294ca849ba7a00d47aa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: James Reilly <jreilly1821@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 04:12:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Persist device keys, one-time keys and the to-device inbox

On a phone the app is killed routinely, and a restart that forgets every
device key and every undelivered room key silently breaks every peer's
Olm session. Four tables now hold the server's share of E2EE as
wire-verbatim JSON: device_keys, one_time_keys, cross_signing_keys and
to_device_inbox. A one-time key is a row, so a claim is a delete rather
than a rewrite of a blob.

Memory stays the runtime copy. Every mutation of the directory or inbox
is journaled to a task that writes it through to the E2eeStore in order;
the whole set is reloaded before serving (serve and router both do it).
Inbox rows carry a caller-assigned id seeded past the loaded maximum, so
memory and disk agree on which rows a drain removes. The cross-signing
and signature-merge paths, which used to poke the maps directly, go
through journaling methods now. Schema version 4.

Tests: the store's round-trip and conflict rules (a device upload
replaces, a one-time key is never replaced, a claim removes exactly its
row, the inbox keeps order and ignores a repeated id), and an HTTP-level
restart: upload, claim one key, receive a room key over federation, then
a fresh application state over the same store finds the device, exactly
the unclaimed key, and the waiting room key, delivered once, with the
drain reaching the store.
